Z||000000||84Bill||09-07-2001||08:05 AM||||Ok road rally fans. How can I get my 84 to stop making furrows in the road when I corner sharply? What I mean is the front just pushes through the corners real bad sometimes especially if I'm powering trough it. What can I do? Add a rear sway bar? <P>Here is a vision<BR>Enter a corner and begin to cut the wheel the car starts to push, add more cut to the wheel and the front wheels start talking louder the change in the cars direction are near nil so add more cut to the wheel, front wheels are screaching like a raped ape but little change in cars track. Let off gas BIG BOOBOO! the front suddenly bites in and the ass end lets loose! stomp on gas and we are back to square one!!!! with the front wheels screaming and plowing again.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> <P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Joe Torma||09-07-2001||11:03 AM||||What kinda stuff are you running...tire size/sway bar/springs.  Changing these will help...low profile tires,stiff springs, and a big sway bar. A different alignment setup may help too.<P>There are a bunch of people that autox here that probably will help.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/simplesig.gif">  <BR>Did you get your car in my book yet????<BR> <A HREF="http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phantom Rage||09-07-2001||11:26 AM||||Add a rear sway bar.||||reg||7
Z||000003||skitime||09-07-2001||11:48 AM||||Try entering the corner under 80 mph.  hehe I would look at what tires you are using.  Low profile tires will gripe better but perhaps the tire is old and hard.  Tread can appear to be good but old tires become hard and the rubber will have less grip.  Swap tire with another car that handles well and see if that is all that is wrong.<P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Butter||09-07-2001||12:14 PM||||84 Bill,<BR>I've improved that problem with the addition of a rear sway and better gripping tires.  It did not solve the problem but then one must remember it is a mid engine car and I've added a little weight in the back with a v8.  Moving the battery up front may be another way to help.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Simple Fiero||09-07-2001||01:19 PM||||how about some weight in the front, or adjusting the toe or camber in the front, sounds like you are not getting any grip under accleration, so what(and I am sure you know this, if you are racing) is happening is all your weight if you acclerate is going to the ass end, how about giving the brakes a little loving nudge just before the corner, I have the same problem on mine but mine happens in the ass end, and in the rain WHEW watch out(bad camber on my part LOL).<P>88 5speed, 4cyl<P>Just trying to understand, and help out!<p>[This message has been edited by Simple Fiero (edited 09-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||84Bill||09-07-2001||03:00 PM||||Thanks for the ideas folks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>There is a large parking lot where I work and when the lot it wet from rain I amaize my coworkers by turning the wheel all the way to the stop and whomp on the gas. I can clear nearly 30 feet before the front starts grabbing enough to cause a directional change  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000007||theogre||09-07-2001||07:37 PM||||I think the usual fix for that is to mess with the camber and maybe castor. For what you are doing, this would likely mean making the car a trailer queen. You'd probably have too much camber/castor for the tires to last long on the street.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||MrPBody||09-07-2001||08:31 PM||||Full-lock turns on wet pavement may represent an unusual performance condition.<P><BR>For less understeer in more normal driving, try:<P>&gt; Enough tire pressure<P>&gt; Slight negative camber front and rear<P>&gt; More caster<P><BR>Then get serious:<P>&gt; Larger anti-roll bar on front, and stock front bar transplanted to rear<P>&gt; Lowering<P>&gt; Better tires<P><BR>Take time to sort-out your car, and re-learn to drive it after modifications.  Don't crash following our advice.  I hate it when that happens.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||84Bill||09-07-2001||10:00 PM||||  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> not to worry, I out grew my feelings of imortality quite a few years ago.<BR>I miss that spell checker<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 09-07-2001).]||||reg||12
Z||000010||ray b||09-07-2001||10:31 PM||||tooo much toe in on the front<BR>for a safe playground to try diffrent bars and sept setings, down load GPL demo sim free<BR>at [Grand Prix Legends]  or at hppt://www.papy.com/gpl/gpl.downloads.index.html<BR>even if you don't have a wheel it will play with a Joy-stick or on the key board<BR>toe in or out, change cambers, and set bars and springs and drive, best tool to learn to set up a mid-eng cars it sims 67 befor wings and good tires itis a real sim not a hero driver game<BR>real F-1 drivers have used it and like it<BR>one warning it is habbit forming .<P><BR>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd<p>[This message has been edited by ray b (edited 09-09-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000011||Fierowrecker||09-07-2001||11:43 PM||||Hey Bill!<BR>What if any mods have been done to the car?<P>I took a bone stock 86 2M4 and improved the cornering by adding a front bar to the rear...<BR>No poly, or other mods, and it was fantastic!<BR>I have added the poly to the front, and it is better, as well as new ball joints upper and lower...<BR>Can't wait to do the rear ball joints and bushings!<BR><P>------------------<BR>crash... The Fierowrecker<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/uploads/crash_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
